  "candidate_prose": "The sea dragon swept through the open lighthouse door, its wet wings nearly brushing the walls. Ekin pressed herself against the dry stone as it crossed the daylight spilling through the upper windows.\n\nThe brass compass hung from the dragon’s jaws.\n\n“Grandfather’s compass,” Ekin whispered.\n\nThe dragon lowered its head over a rough nest beside the wall and placed the compass among the twisted reeds. Sunlight flashed on its scratched lid. Ekin saw it clearly, only a few quick steps away.\n\nShe started forward.\n\n“Wait,” Duru said.\n\nA small shape stirred inside the nest. Duru noticed it first and pointed. Ekin followed his finger.\n\nA sea dragon hatchling, no larger than a cat, pushed its head above the reeds. Its little wings opened and fluttered, but they could not lift it. Unhurt, it clambered beside the compass.\n\nThe mother dragon swung around. She planted herself between the children and the nest, wings half spread, blocking the approach. Ekin stopped as the dragon’s claws scraped the floor.\n\nAt the other side of the upper room, a narrow turning stair led down toward the lower maintenance room. Ekin and Duru remained on the upper-room floor and looked down its tight curve. Through the stair opening, they could both see a fixed iron grate in the sea-facing wall below. Gray light and harbor water gleamed between its bars.\n\nDuru glanced from the stair to the mother dragon. “Don’t rush her.”\n\nEkin’s hands curled into fists inside her jacket pockets. He was right that the nest was blocked. She could see that perfectly well.\n\nBut she could also see the compass shining beside the hatchling.\n\n“It’s right there,” she said, sharper than she meant. “I’m not leaving it.”",
